WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.880
It: Chapter 2 has finally pranced into theaters,
bringing the long, bloody saga of the Losers

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.100
Club to a close.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.260
But we're not leaving the circus just yet.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.560
With a nearly three-hour runtime and a thousand
page book to reference, there were bound to

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.840
be plenty of Easter eggs in It: Chapter 2.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.920
Here are the ones you might have missed.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.780
It: Chapter 2 had a lot of catching up to
do.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:25.250
While the first film focused exclusively on
the kids, this one had the dual job of both

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.320
introducing the adult versions of the Losers
and making you believe that they really could

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.240
be those children all grown up.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.860
When we're introduced to adult Ben Hanscom,
we see a board room with a bunch of men arguing

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.140
over the model of a building.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.900
After the camera slides over the room, it
finally focuses on one man.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:41.680
It's Ben!

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.160
Except...it's not.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.500
Ben, as portrayed by Jay Ryan, is on a Skype
call with everybody in that room.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.460
But it's a double fake-out, because that first
guy was Ben all grown up.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.460
Confused?

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:57.340
See, that actor was Brandon Crane, who played
Ben Hanscom in the 1990 miniseries.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.340
In a story all about childhood, loss of childhood,
and facing the fears of your childhood, giving

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.170
an actor the chance to play both a child and
an adult in an adaptation of It is basically

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.160
poetry.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.800
Speaking of cameos, there was a special one
that fans of Stephen King probably noticed,

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.380
but which might have flown over the heads
of casual viewers.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:19.720
No, not the one where director Andy Muschietti
appeared in the background when Eddie visits

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.860
the Derry pharmacy.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.340
We're talking, of course, about King himself.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.780
The horror auteur showed up as the owner of
Secondhand Rose, the thrift store where Bill

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.760
finds Silver, his childhood bike.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.860
Like King's portrayal in the film, the proprietor
in the novel is kind of a jerk.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.280
"Sonny!

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.380
Come on over here, sugar buns."

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.980
But that's not the end.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.000
In the film, the shopkeeper has a copy of
Bill's book Attic Room on his desk.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.460
After Bill notices the book, King repeats
what everyone else says about Bill's books:

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:49.920
he doesn't like the ending.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.680
It's a joke that's overused in the movie,
but hearing the criticism coming from King

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.460
himself is a pretty great nod to the fact
that a whole lot of readers don't like the

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.160
endings of King's books.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.860
Because let's face it: they're not always
great.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:08.900
One character the films leave out entirely
is Maturin, an ancient intergalactic turtle

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.680
that sort of helps the Losers defeat It during
round one of their face-off.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.860
In the climactic scene in the kids' timeline,
Bill performs the Ritual of Chud with Pennywise

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.510
and ends up soaring through a cosmic realm
beyond space and time, at which point he encounters

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.760
a big ol' turtle.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.419
Maturin comes from the same dimension as the
being known as It, and in fact created our

00:02:28.419 --> 00:02:31.159
world by puking it out when he had indigestion.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.580
So...good thing the Macroverse doesn't have
Pepto Bismol.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:39.279
While neither of the It films have dared to
quite go into that level of cosmic craziness,

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.780
they've definitely dropped a few turtle Easter
eggs.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:46.100
It: Chapter 1 put a Lego turtle in Georgie's
room, as well as a mention of a turtle at

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.960
the quarry where the kids go swimming.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.320
In Chapter 2, we see a model of a turtle at
Derry Elementary when Ben revisits the school

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.520
to find his token.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.220
Are there more scattered around the film?

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.620
We wouldn't be surprised.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.040
In an effort to double down on Bill's guilt
at not being able to save his little brother,

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:04.879
Chapter 2 also brings in another kid named
Dean to die right in front of Bill despite

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.500
his best efforts to save him.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:11.340
The kid shows up three times in the film,
but in the novel he only appears once, when

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.559
Bill is wandering around Derry and revisits
the old sewer opening where Georgie became

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:16.940
Pennywise's lunch.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.080
Bill notices the kid with his skateboard and
urges him to stay away from the sewers because

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.760
there are killer clowns afoot.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.219
If you pay close attention to the underside
of Dean's skateboard, you can see that, although

00:03:27.219 --> 00:03:31.749
it's a little roughed up, it once had the
same design as the Overlook Hotel's carpet

00:03:31.749 --> 00:03:34.280
in Stanley Kubrick's adaptation of The Shining.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.279
When the adult Losers finally track Pennywise
down to its underground lair and totally flub

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.580
the Ritual of Chud, the clown gets them all
to scatter.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.900
One by one, they all relive a twisted, surreal
version of their childhood fears.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.920
Beverly's nightmare takes on the form of a
stall in a school bathroom, one that looks

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.780
a lot like the one where she got trash dumped
on her in the beginning of the first film.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.040
To her, the stall clearly symbolizes some
sort of cage, because her tormentors from

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.960
over the years begin trying to burst through
the door to taunt her.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:06.499
They all have something relevant to say except
crazy Henry Bowers, who jams his head into

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.800
the gap and simply shouts the most iconic
line from The Shining:

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.720
"Here's Johnny."

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.020
Tim Curry's rendition of Pennywise in the
1990 miniseries sent many a kid to bed with

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:21.400
nightmares of toothy clowns coming out of
the locker room drain.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.800
Yeah, it's a little goofy these days, but
you can only do so much with a made-for-TV

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.080
special effects budget.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.280
While it's not the most fondly remembered
Stephen King adaptation, the 1990 version

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.840
of It definitely has its own place in history.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.180
For about 27 years, Tim Curry was the definitive
creepy clown, and no modern adaptation worth

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.360
its salted kid meat is going to ignore it
completely.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:47.319
In It: Chapter 1, we got to see a clown with
a striking resemblance to Tim Curry's Pennywise

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.540
in the scene where Richie explores the house
on Neibolt Street.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:54.060
And in the sequel, we got even more nods to
the miniseries.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.700
For starters, one of the most iconic lines
made a return in the 2019 film.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.699
During the flashback of young Ben Hanscom
hiding from It in his locker after trying

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:06.680
to kiss Beverly, Pennywise pops up with one
of the most well-known taunts from the 1990

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:07.600
version.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.820
You know the one.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.420
"Kiss me, fat boy."

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:17.259
And at the Canal Days festival, Bill chases
a young boy into a clown-themed funhouse and

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:20.030
runs into a room full of swinging clown bags.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:24.610
Look closely, and you can see that those clowns
have the same pattern as the outfit from Curry's

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.680
Pennywise, right down to the orange pom poms
and curvy eyebrows.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.560
Stephen King's novel spent multiple chapters
hammering home the point that although the

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.740
Losers grew up physically, they each carried
remnants of their childhood into adulthood

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:38.480
with them.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.960
In Eddie's case, he grew up into a man who
couldn't quite bear to leave his mother behind.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.110
So he married her.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.710
Okay, not literally, but Eddie's wife Myra
is physically, and sometimes spiritually,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:50.260
a doppelganger of his mother.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:55.580
She's big, needy, domineering, and fixated
on her husband's welfare to the point of emotionally

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:56.680
traumatizing him.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:57.880
Just like mom!

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:01.800
"My mom will have an aneurysm, okay, if she
finds out we're playing down here.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:02.800
I'm serious."

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:08.060
In It: Chapter 2, we only saw Myra Kaspbrak
briefly, but sharp-eyed viewers may have noticed

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.349
a very, very specific detail.

00:06:10.349 --> 00:06:15.020
Namely, she's portrayed by the same actress
who plays Eddie's mother in both films, Molly

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:15.980
Atkinson.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:19.440
So if you got a weird feeling in your stomach
thinking that Eddie's wife looked a little

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.820
too much like his mom, you weren't wrong.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:26.880
If you had to name two mainstream horror authors
from the past few decades, chances are you'd

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.600
be quick to come up with two names: Stephen
King, and Dean Koontz.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:34.560
Both are prolific writers, and both have been
writing published fiction for about the same

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:35.400
length of time.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.159
Obviously, the two authors have taken notice
of each other over the years, and while they

00:06:39.159 --> 00:06:43.210
probably don't have the kind of rivalry fans
think they do, they haven't been averse to

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.360
taking shots at each other over the years.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.200
Maybe that's why King wrote Koontz into It
as an orderly at Juniper Hill Asylum who watches

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.700
over an incarcerated Henry Bowers.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:56.460
That scene where he's introduced involves
an observation from the perspective of Henry

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.300
Bowers that, quote, "Koontz is the worst."

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:03.960
In It: Chapter 2, Koontz is alive and well,
with an especially fitting appearance.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.800
He's an employee at Juniper Hill, and you
can spot him in the film as the guard watching

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.920
a dog video on his phone when Henry Bowers
breaks out.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.780
Because of course he is.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.460
"Dogs are magical.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.300
They're a lot more magical than elves."

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:21.720
In the book and, to a lesser extent, It: Chapter
1, It often takes the shape of movie monsters

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.140
to terrify the kids.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:26.819
Ben Hanscom is scared of The Mummy, while
Richie's fears take the form of the hairy

00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:31.700
shapeshifter from I Was a Teenage Werewolf
and the oozing peeper from a 1958 creature

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:33.500
feature called The Crawling Eye.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.560
Because who wouldn't be scared of that?

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:40.100
But with updated times come updated scares,
and it appears that these '80s kids found

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.200
some new monster movies to be scared of.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:46.280
Something like John Carpenter's 1982 horror
masterpiece The Thing.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:51.160
For a refresher, The Thing is about a shapeshifting
alien that plagues a research team in Antarctica.

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:55.710
It can take the form of anything it infects,
but it has to go through hideous transformations

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:56.820
to get there.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:01.500
In one of It: Chapter 2's more memorable scares,
Richie, Bill, and Eddie come across Stan's

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.740
decapitated head in a fridge inside the house
at 29 Neibolt Street.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:10.440
Almost immediately, the head grows a bunch
of freaky spider legs and Richie says, "You

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.180
gotta be f----ing kidding me."

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:16.219
It's a fitting tribute to the scene in Carpenter's
horror fest, which sees one of the researchers

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:20.960
lose his head to the tentacled monstrosity,
after which Palmer says, you guessed it:

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:28.400
"You gotta be f----ing kidding."

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:33.540
Later, The Thing seemingly gets referenced
once again when that adorable Pomeranian behind

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:38.159
the "Not Scary" door shows up and turns into
a twisted dog mutant that looks a lot like

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.380
the dog that got infected in The Thing.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:45.880
Probably the creepiest sequence in the 2017
film was when the kids go exploring in the

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:50.400
house on Neibolt Street and run into a variety
of clown-flavored horror spectacles.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.600
Certainly the most memorable segment from
that sees Richie and Bill faced with the "Scary

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:55.540
doors."

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:59.940
Thinking on their feet, they first run to
the door labeled "Not Scary At All," and find

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:01.860
that, surprise, it's pretty scary.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:05.720
Inside they see one of the missing girls hanging
by her hands with everything below her waist

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:06.720
missing.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.640
"Where the f--- were her legs?"

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:13.060
Well, it takes 27 years, but Richie eventually
finds those legs.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:17.060
When they're back in the Neibolt house as
adults, Richie again comes up against the

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:21.630
three scary doors, and the first one he opens
reveals a pair of torso-less legs skipping

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:22.500
towards him.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.540
Guess it goes to show: be careful what you
wish for.

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:29.900
Technically, this Easter egg breaks through
the universe of the film and into our world,

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.460
which is even creepier.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.720
Okay, so it's not that creepy, but it's still
pretty cool.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:40.000
Most King fans know that he's big on the number
27, and It in particular uses the number as

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.960
a main plot point; Pennywise comes back every
27 years.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:49.280
It makes sense then that It: Chapter 2 saw
a release date of September 6, 2019.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:55.180
If you break 9/6/2019 down number by number,
all the digits add up to 27.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.860
That's not a coincidence, either.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:02.509
It: Chapter 1, released on September 8, 2017,
follows the same formula: all the digits in

00:10:02.509 --> 00:10:06.920
the date 9/8/2017 equal exactly 27 as well.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:11.720
Add to that the fact that the first film was
released 27 years after the 1990 miniseries,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.020
and, well, things are starting to get a little
freaky here.

